NNAAMMEE
       time_namespaces - overview of Linux time namespaces

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       Time namespaces virtualize the values of two system clocks:

       •  CCLLOOCCKK__MMOONNOOTTOONNIICC  (and  likewise CCLLOOCCKK__MMOONNOOTTOONNIICC__CCOOAARRSSEE and CCLLOOCCKK__MMOO‐‐
          NNOOTTOONNIICC__RRAAWW), a nonsettable clock  that  represents  monotonic  time
          since —as described by POSIX— "some unspecified point in the past".

       •  CCLLOOCCKK__BBOOOOTTTTIIMMEE  (and  likewise  CCLLOOCCKK__BBOOOOTTTTIIMMEE__AALLAARRMM), a nonsettable
          clock that is identical to CCLLOOCCKK__MMOONNOOTTOONNIICC, except that it also  in‐
          cludes any time that the system is suspended.

       Thus,  the processes in a time namespace share per‐namespace values for
       these clocks.  This affects various APIs  that  measure  against  these
       clocks,  including: cclloocckk__ggeettttiimmee(2), cclloocckk__nnaannoosslleeeepp(2), nnaannoosslleeeepp(2),
       ttiimmeerr__sseettttiimmee(2), ttiimmeerrffdd__sseettttiimmee(2), and _/_p_r_o_c_/_u_p_t_i_m_e.

       Currently, the only way to create a time namespace is  by  calling  uunn‐‐
       sshhaarree(2)  with  the  CCLLOONNEE__NNEEWWTTIIMMEE  flag.  This call creates a new time
       namespace but does _n_o_t place the calling process in the new  namespace.
       Instead, the calling process’s subsequently created children are placed
       in  the  new  namespace.  This allows clock offsets (see below) for the
       new namespace to be set before the first process is placed in the name‐
       space.  The _/_p_r_o_c_/pid_/_n_s_/_t_i_m_e___f_o_r___c_h_i_l_d_r_e_n symbolic link shows the time
       namespace in which the children of  a  process  will  be  created.   (A
       process  can  use  a  file descriptor opened on this symbolic link in a
       call to sseettnnss(2) in order to move into the namespace.)

   _//_pp_rr_oo_cc_//ppiidd_//_tt_ii_mm_ee_nn_ss____oo_ff_ff_ss_ee_tt_ss
       Associated with each time namespace are offsets, expressed with respect
       to the initial time namespace, that define the values of the  monotonic
       and  boot‐time clocks in that namespace.  These offsets are exposed via
       the file _/_p_r_o_c_/pid_/_t_i_m_e_n_s___o_f_f_s_e_t_s.  Within this file, the  offsets  are
       expressed as lines consisting of three space‐delimited fields:

           <clock‐id> <offset‐secs> <offset‐nanosecs>

       The  _c_l_o_c_k_‐_i_d  is  a string that identifies the clock whose offsets are
       being shown.  This field is either _m_o_n_o_t_o_n_i_c, for  CCLLOOCCKK__MMOONNOOTTOONNIICC,  or
       _b_o_o_t_t_i_m_e,  for CCLLOOCCKK__BBOOOOTTTTIIMMEE.  The remaining fields express the offset
       (seconds plus nanoseconds) for the clock in this time namespace.  These
       offsets are expressed relative to the clock values in the initial  time
       namespace.   The _o_f_f_s_e_t_‐_s_e_c_s value can be negative, subject to restric‐
       tions noted below; _o_f_f_s_e_t_‐_n_a_n_o_s_e_c_s is an unsigned value.

       In the initial time namespace, the contents of the _t_i_m_e_n_s___o_f_f_s_e_t_s  file
       are as follows:

           $ ccaatt //pprroocc//sseellff//ttiimmeennss__ooffffsseettss
           monotonic           0         0
           boottime            0         0

       In  a  new  time  namespace that has had no member processes, the clock
       offsets can be modified by writing newline‐terminated  records  of  the
       same  form to the _t_i_m_e_n_s___o_f_f_s_e_t_s file.  The file can be written to mul‐
       tiple times, but after the first process has been created in or has en‐
       tered the namespace, wwrriittee(2)s on this file fail with the error EEAACCCCEESS.
       In order to write to the _t_i_m_e_n_s___o_f_f_s_e_t_s file, a process must  have  the
       CCAAPP__SSYYSS__TTIIMMEE  capability in the user namespace that owns the time name‐
       space.

       Writes to the _t_i_m_e_n_s___o_f_f_s_e_t_s file can fail with the following errors:

       EEIINNVVAALL An _o_f_f_s_e_t_‐_n_a_n_o_s_e_c_s value is greater than 999,999,999.

       EEIINNVVAALL A _c_l_o_c_k_‐_i_d value is not valid.

       EEPPEERRMM  The caller does not have the CCAAPP__SSYYSS__TTIIMMEE capability.

       EERRAANNGGEE An _o_f_f_s_e_t_‐_s_e_c_s value is out of range.  In particular;

              •  _o_f_f_s_e_t_‐_s_e_c_s can’t be set to a value which would make the cur‐
                 rent time on the corresponding clock inside the  namespace  a
                 negative value; and

              •  _o_f_f_s_e_t_‐_s_e_c_s can’t be set to a value such that the time on the
                 corresponding clock inside the namespace would exceed half of
                 the  value  of the kernel constant KKTTIIMMEE__SSEECC__MMAAXX (this limits
                 the clock value to a maximum of approximately 146 years).

       In a new time namespace created by  uunnsshhaarree(2),  the  contents  of  the
       _t_i_m_e_n_s___o_f_f_s_e_t_s file are inherited from the time namespace of the creat‐
       ing process.

NNOOTTEESS
       Use  of  time  namespaces requires a kernel that is configured with the
       CCOONNFFIIGG__TTIIMMEE__NNSS option.

       Note that time namespaces do not virtualize the  CCLLOOCCKK__RREEAALLTTIIMMEE  clock.
       Virtualization  of this clock was avoided for reasons of complexity and
       overhead within the kernel.

       For compatibility with  the  initial  implementation,  when  writing  a
       _c_l_o_c_k_‐_i_d  to the _/_p_r_o_c_/pid_/_t_i_m_e_n_s___o_f_f_s_e_t_s file, the numerical values of
       the IDs can be written instead of the symbolic names show above;  i.e.,
       1  instead  of  _m_o_n_o_t_o_n_i_c, and 7 instead of _b_o_o_t_t_i_m_e.  For readability,
       the use of the symbolic names over the numbers is preferred.

       The motivation for adding time namespaces was to  allow  the  monotonic
       and boot‐time clocks to maintain consistent values during container mi‐
       gration and checkpoint/restore.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
       The  following  shell  session demonstrates the operation of time name‐
       spaces.  We begin by displaying the inode number of the time  namespace
       of a shell in the initial time namespace:

           $ rreeaaddlliinnkk //pprroocc//$$$$//nnss//ttiimmee
           time:[4026531834]

       Continuing  in the initial time namespace, we display the system uptime
       using uuppttiimmee(1) and  use  the  _c_l_o_c_k___t_i_m_e_s  example  program  shown  in
       cclloocckk__ggeettrreess(2) to display the values of various clocks:

           $ uuppttiimmee ----pprreettttyy
           up 21 hours, 17 minutes
           $ ..//cclloocckk__ttiimmeess
           CLOCK_REALTIME : 1585989401.971 (18356 days +  8h 36m 41s)
           CLOCK_TAI      : 1585989438.972 (18356 days +  8h 37m 18s)
           CLOCK_MONOTONIC:      56338.247 (15h 38m 58s)
           CLOCK_BOOTTIME :      76633.544 (21h 17m 13s)

       We then use uunnsshhaarree(1) to create a time namespace and execute a bbaasshh(1)
       shell.   From  the new shell, we use the built‐in eecchhoo command to write
       records to  the  _t_i_m_e_n_s___o_f_f_s_e_t_s  file  adjusting  the  offset  for  the
       CCLLOOCCKK__MMOONNOOTTOONNIICC clock forward 2 days and the offset for the CCLLOOCCKK__BBOOOOTT‐‐
       TTIIMMEE clock forward 7 days:

           $ PPSS11==ns2# ssuuddoo uunnsshhaarree --TT ---- bbaasshh ----nnoorrcc
           ns2# eecchhoo monotonic$$((((22**2244**6600**6600))))0">>/proc/$$/timens_offsets"
           ns2# eecchhoo boottime$$((((77**2244**6600**6600))))0">>/proc/$$/timens_offsets"

       Above,  we  started the bbaasshh(1) shell with the ----nnoorrcc option so that no
       start‐up scripts were executed.  This ensures that no  child  processes
       are  created  from  the  shell  before  we  have a chance to update the
       _t_i_m_e_n_s___o_f_f_s_e_t_s file.

       We then use ccaatt(1) to display the contents of the _t_i_m_e_n_s___o_f_f_s_e_t_s  file.
       The execution of ccaatt(1) creates the first process in the new time name‐
       space,  after  which further attempts to update the _t_i_m_e_n_s___o_f_f_s_e_t_s file
       produce an error.

           ns2# ccaatt //pprroocc//$$$$//ttiimmeennss__ooffffsseettss
           monotonic      172800         0
           boottime       604800         0
           ns2# eecchhoo ""bboooottttiimmee $$((((99**2244**6600**6600)))) 00"" >> //pprroocc//$$$$//ttiimmeennss__ooffffsseettss
           bash: echo: write error: Permission denied

       Continuing  in  the  new  namespace,  we  execute  uuppttiimmee(1)  and   the
       _c_l_o_c_k___t_i_m_e_s example program:

           ns2# uuppttiimmee ----pprreettttyy
           up 1 week, 21 hours, 18 minutes
           ns2# ..//cclloocckk__ttiimmeess
           CLOCK_REALTIME : 1585989457.056 (18356 days +  8h 37m 37s)
           CLOCK_TAI      : 1585989494.057 (18356 days +  8h 38m 14s)
           CLOCK_MONOTONIC:     229193.332 (2 days + 15h 39m 53s)
           CLOCK_BOOTTIME :     681488.629 (7 days + 21h 18m  8s)

       From  the  above  output,  we  can see that the monotonic and boot‐time
       clocks have different values in the new time namespace.

       Examining the _/_p_r_o_c_/pid_/_n_s_/_t_i_m_e and _/_p_r_o_c_/pid_/_n_s_/_t_i_m_e___f_o_r___c_h_i_l_d_r_e_n sym‐
       bolic links, we see that the shell is a  member  of  the  initial  time
       namespace, but its children are created in the new namespace.

           ns2# rreeaaddlliinnkk //pprroocc//$$$$//nnss//ttiimmee
           time:[4026531834]
           ns2# rreeaaddlliinnkk //pprroocc//$$$$//nnss//ttiimmee__ffoorr__cchhiillddrreenn
           time:[4026532900]
           ns2# rreeaaddlliinnkk //pprroocc//sseellff//nnss//ttiimmee  # Creates a child process
           time:[4026532900]

       Returning  to  the shell in the initial time namespace, we see that the
       monotonic and boot‐time clocks are  unaffected  by  the  _t_i_m_e_n_s___o_f_f_s_e_t_s
       changes that were made in the other time namespace:

           $ uuppttiimmee ----pprreettttyy
           up 21 hours, 19 minutes
           $ ..//cclloocckk__ttiimmeess
           CLOCK_REALTIME : 1585989401.971 (18356 days +  8h 38m 51s)
           CLOCK_TAI      : 1585989438.972 (18356 days +  8h 39m 28s)
           CLOCK_MONOTONIC:      56338.247 (15h 41m  8s)
           CLOCK_BOOTTIME :      76633.544 (21h 19m 23s)

